 ## Overview
-This article defines the specific hyperparameters, network architectures, and loss functions used to train the Mip-NeRF 360 model. It solves the problem of stabilizing the optimization of unbounded, anti-aliased radiance fields by combining a multi-stage proposal sampling strategy with a robust reconstruction loss. It differs from standard NeRF training by enforcing a strict separation between proposal network optimization and NeRF optimization via stop-gradient operations, and by using a Charbonnier loss instead of MSE to handle outliers in "in-the-wild" imagery [S1].
+This article specifies the hyperparameters, network architectures, and loss functions used to train the Mip-NeRF 360 model [S1]. It stabilizes optimization by combining a two-stage proposal sampling strategy with a Charbonnier reconstruction loss and a stop-gradient operation that decouples proposal network updates from NeRF updates [S1]. It differs from standard NeRF training by enforcing strict separation between proposal and NeRF optimization via stop-gradient operations, and by using a Charbonnier loss instead of mean squared error to achieve more stable optimization [S1].
 
